#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180381} :activity/id #uuid "bfa29f8b-fff6-5ca4-9fa7-be694c7b6715" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049629 :topic/id #uuid "06d0667d-f4ac-5187-8004-79d10b9d353a" :topic/math-academy-id 3697} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180381 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180675 :tutorial/content "A partition $P$ of a closed interval $[a,b]$ is a finite sequence of real numbers taken from $[a,b]$ such that\n\n$$\na = x_0 <      x_1<      x_2 <      \\ldots <      x_n = b\\,.\n$$\n\nFor example, given the interval $[0,6],$ one possible partition is \n\n$$\n0 < 2 < 5 < 6.\n$$\n\nWe can express this partition using set notation as\n\n$$\nP=\\{0,2,5,6\\}.\n$$\n\nNote that this partition breaks up the interval $[0,6]$ into the union of the following subintervals:\n\n$$\n[0,2] \\cup [2,5] \\cup [5,6]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "874ce92b-031e-55f5-92e4-5883ab9f029a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3874 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ef5d4a8f-ff27-5806-af27-4b7ffb16f3c0" :step/math-academy-id 22785 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180465}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179582 :question/id #uuid "2574b91a-6534-562b-b4dc-8d95fd19e169" :question/math-academy-id "e-9480"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c91d51f-39b4-547a-971b-9c2dada9eb47" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048922}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523942} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565470}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Simple Sums Over Partitions of the Real Line"} :step/id #uuid "adc61151-e0b6-5426-832a-962b7f89c76e" :step/math-academy-id 35481 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180462}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180462 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179721 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179583 :question/id #uuid "8f09771b-3e0d-567b-a39f-f877bff53372" :question/math-academy-id "e-9481"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f55bdc9-4066-5786-bafe-c3fdc96a06c9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049943}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256768} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523933} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186523983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565453} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565472}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Sums Over Partitions of the Real Line"} :step/id #uuid "589bd944-74be-586b-b8aa-de1f404e6b97" :step/math-academy-id 35489} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180464 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180706 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the partition $P=\\{0,2,5,6\\}$ of the interval $[a,b]=[0,6].$ Then, we have\n\n$$\na=\\underbrace{0}_{\\large x_0}   <   \\underbrace{2}_{\\large x_1}   <  \\underbrace{5}_{\\large x_2} <  \\underbrace{6}_{\\large x_3} =b.\n$$\n\nThis partition breaks up the interval $[0,6]$ into the union of the following subintervals:\n\n$$\n[0,2] \\cup [2,5] \\cup [5,6]\n$$\n\nThe length of the $i$th subinterval, denoted $\\Delta x_i,$ is simply the difference of the endpoints: \n\n- The length of the first interval $[0,2],$ denoted $\\Delta x_1,$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{x}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-{x}_{0} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The length of the second interval $[2,5],$ denoted $\\Delta x_2,$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{x}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-{x}_{1} \\\\  & =5-2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The length of the third interval $[5,6],$ denoted $\\Delta x_3,$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{x}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-{x}_{2} \\\\  & =6-5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{3}Δ{x}_{i} & =Δ{x}_{1}+Δ{x}_{2}+Δ{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2+3+1 \\\\  & =6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is just the length of the interval $[0, 6].$\n\n**Note:** In general, for any partition $P=\\{x_0,x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$ of an interval $[a,b],$ the sum \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^m\\Delta x_i\n$$\n\n is just the length of the interval $[a, b],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =Δ{x}_{1}+Δ{x}_{2}+Δ{x}_{3}+⋯+Δ{x}_{m} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})+({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})+({x}_{3}-{x}_{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}-{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}}-{x}_{0})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{x}_{3}}-\\cancel{{x}_{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}+{x}_{m} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}-{x}_{0} \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "714f6267-47f5-5ba8-a3e7-3b7ef319e601" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5779 :tutorial/title "Sums Over Partitions"} :step/id #uuid "866321d3-59e7-5859-b4e4-2adecd40dfc1" :step/math-academy-id 35493 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180461}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180465 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179723 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179585 :question/id #uuid "eff8c72f-429d-583a-aa22-f7786f09262a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9484"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2bc800b0-3901-5154-81ac-1ef853017320" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049162}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396627} {:db/id #edb/ref 175921865